# Binary Lifting Table Example

Suppose

```
f[][0]

A→B

B→C

C→D

D→D
```

Then

```
f[][1]

A→C

B→D

C→D

D→D
```

Then

```
f[][2]

A→D

B→D

C→D

D→D
```

So one lookup gives

```
4 jumps
```

---

# Why This Works

Sorting transforms the graph into an ordered structure where every optimal move is toward the farthest reachable value within `maxDiff`. The two-pointer technique computes this greedy next jump for every node. Binary lifting then composes these jumps into powers of two, allowing each query to skip many intermediate nodes while still finding the minimum number of jumps or determining that no path exists. This works because moving farther within the allowed range can never reduce future reachability in the sorted order.

